---
import type { TocItem } from '../consts';
import PageLayout from '../layouts/PageLayout.astro';

const toc: TocItem[] = [
  { id: 'result', label: 'Published result' },
  { id: 'design', label: 'What was compared' },
  { id: 'failures', label: 'What the score reveals' },
  { id: 'meaning', label: 'What it means' },
  { id: 'evidence', label: 'Evidence and reproduction' },
];
---

<PageLayout
  title="Evaluation"
  description="Published evidence from a 648-session fixed-panel comparison of three configurations for complete Welch-report verification."
  toc={toc}
>
  <header class="page-head">
    <p class="eyebrow">Evaluation</p>
    <h1>From recalculating a test to checking the whole submission</h1>
    <p class="standfirst">
      A 648-session study tested whether three tool-assisted configurations could return
      the complete expected assessment of a Welch report—not just a plausible number.
    </p>
  </header>

  <section id="result" class="section">
    <p class="eyebrow">Published result</p>
    <h2>The bound verifier returned 216 of 216 expected reports</h2>
    <div class="score-grid">
      <article>
        <p class="arm-label">B · General Python</p>
        <p class="score">132 / 216</p>
        <p>61.11% complete-report correctness</p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <p class="arm-label">W · Stable Welch wrapper</p>
        <p class="score">131 / 216</p>
        <p>60.65% complete-report correctness</p>
      </article>
      <article class="featured">
        <p class="arm-label">C · Bound verifier</p>
        <p class="score">216 / 216</p>
        <p>100% on this fixed constructed panel</p>
      </article>
    </div>
    <p class="primary">
      <strong>The measured outcome is exact correctness of a five-field submission report.</strong>
      These rates are not estimates of general research accuracy. They describe one fixed
      model, one constructed panel, and the complete configurations tested in September 2026.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="design" class="section">
    <h2>What was compared</h2>
    <p>
      The experiment used 72 newly generated questions in nine predefined Welch-report
      families. Each question was run three times in each of three configurations, for
      648 independently scored sessions. All configurations used
      <code>gpt-4.1-mini-2025-04-14</code>, the same case surface, and the same session
      limits. Expected outcomes were fixed independently of the scored model output.
    </p>
    <div class="arms">
      <article>
        <p class="arm-label">B · Control</p>
        <h3>General calculation tools</h3>
        <p>The model used Python and assembled the five-field report itself.</p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <p class="arm-label">W · Numerical wrapper</p>
        <h3>Stable Welch calculation</h3>
        <p>A stable wrapper supplied the calculation, while the model still assembled the report.</p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <p class="arm-label">C · Bound verifier</p>
        <h3>Submission-level assessment</h3>
        <p>An argument-free tool call returned the assessment bound to the submitted data and conditions.</p>
      </article>
    </div>
    <p>
      This design compares complete configurations. C changes binding, field provenance,
      and structured return together, so the study does not identify a binding-only causal
      effect. C's result path was deterministic and confirmed before the live run; its
      216/216 result is not evidence of stochastic model stability.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="failures" class="section">
    <h2>What the score reveals</h2>
    <p>
      A correct calculation can still produce an incorrect submission assessment. The
      five scored fields cover the overall verdict, numerical correctness, named error
      fields, missing fields, and conflicts between requested and submitted conditions.
      The largest differences appeared in cases with missing information or undefined
      inference, where the correct response was not a conventional numeric result.
    </p>
    <p>
      Adding a stable Welch wrapper did not improve whole-report correctness on this
      panel: W scored 131/216, compared with B's 132/216. This does not show that numerical
      stability is unhelpful. The panel did not contain near-boundary numerical cases that
      could detect such a benefit; its numerical discrepancies were deliberately large.
    </p>
    <p>
      Output acceptance also mattered. Fifteen B sessions and one W session returned the
      five fields as bare JSON rather than inside the required wrapper. A sensitivity
      analysis accepting that form raised B to 142/216 (65.74%). It did not change the
      qualitative comparison with C.
    </p>
    <p class="actions"><a href="/evaluation/cases/">See three cases and all three repetitions</a></p>
  </section>

  <section id="meaning" class="section">
    <h2>What the result means—and what it does not</h2>
    <p>
      The evidence supports a bounded claim: for these 72 constructed Welch-report
      questions and this fixed model, the bound-verifier configuration produced the
      expected complete assessments more reliably than configurations in which the model
      assembled the final report.
    </p>
    <p>It does not establish that:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>binding alone caused the difference;</li>
      <li>nomue is 100% accurate on real research submissions or other statistical methods;</li>
      <li>the stable numerical wrapper has no value near floating-point boundaries;</li>
      <li>B and W lacked all recognition of missing or undefined information; or</li>
      <li>another model, prompt, task distribution, or future version will reproduce the same rates.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      The study was developer-led, not preregistered, and has not been peer reviewed.
      Tasuku Kobayashi is Licklider's founder and CEO. Kaori Saito, PhD in Statistics,
      reviewed the calculations, scoring endpoint, oracle independence, evidence integrity,
      and claim limits, but did not rerun the live provider sessions. Generative AI assisted
      drafting, checking, and adversarial review; that work is not independent peer review.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="evidence" class="section">
    <h2>Evidence and reproduction</h2>
    <p>
      The preprint and reproducibility package are archived together on Zenodo. The package
      contains the 648 scored rows, panel inputs and expected outputs, reference calculations,
      aggregation and checking code, integrity manifests, saved tool definitions, and frozen
      source material.
    </p>
    <p class="actions">
      <a href="https://zenodo.org/records/22784595">Open the Zenodo record</a>
      <a href="/research/whole-submission-welch-verification/">Read the publication note</a>
    </p>
    <p>
      The saved evidence can be rescored without provider calls. The private C verifier is
      not included, so the original C sessions cannot be rerun independently from the public
      package. Reproduction of the archived results and a live replication are different claims.
    </p>
  </section>

  <footer class="page-foot">
    <p>Published September 16, 2026 · Preprint v1.0 · Not peer reviewed</p>
    <p><a href="/nomue/">How nomue works</a></p>
  </footer>
</PageLayout>

